{
  "description": "The hold rule on the active controller (DESIGN v2.7 C): what each press or release does to the voice capture, and which control owns the running hold. A tracker starts idle, with no owner, and the steps play in order. A release ends the capture only when its control is the owner, whatever that control is mapped to now; every other release is ignored. A press of a non-hold action delivers its intent (or is ignored for none) and leaves the owner alone. A hold press while the controller owns a capture cancels it and clears the owner; while a toggle-started capture runs (toggleCaptureActive) it is ignored; otherwise it begins a capture in the action's mode and records the control as the owner. releaseAll ends a controller-owned capture (never cancels it) and is otherwise ignored. Input is {steps}, each step either {action: <action descriptor>, control, phase: \"began\" or \"ended\", toggleCaptureActive} or {releaseAll: true}. Expected is {effects, owner}: one effect descriptor per step, and the owner left at the end (null for none). An effect descriptor is {effect: beginCapture, endCapture, cancelCapture, deliver or ignore}, plus mode (send or draft) for beginCapture and intent (an intent descriptor) for deliver.",
  "function": "HoldTracker.effect(for:control:phase:toggleCaptureActive:) and releaseAll() — input {steps}, expected {effects, owner}",
  "verifiedBy": "DESIGN.md v2.7 C; Tests/PowerUpTests/DeviceFixtureTests.swift testHoldRuleTable plays the steps through one HoldTracker (which asks the core through the bridge since slice 2.2) and compares the effects and the owner. Rust: rust/powerup-core/tests/device.rs hold_rule_cases plays them through hold::HoldState::step and release_all, passing the state from step to step.",
  "capturedAgainst": "Swift 5.9.2 (Xcode 15.2), PowerUp at 6255e60",
  "cases": [
    {
